The '''2010–11 season''' was the 117th season of competitive [[association football|football]] by [[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]]. New manager [[Neil Lennon]] made considerable changes to the Celtic team for the 2010–11 season. He sold [[Aiden McGeady]] for a then Scottish record £9.5 million<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/football/spl/2010/08/13/aiden-mcgeady-puts-pen-to-paper-on-9-5m-switch-to-spartak-moscow-86908-22485224/ |title=Aiden McGeady puts pen to paper on £9.5m switch to Spartak Moscow|work=Daily Record|date=13 August 2010 |access-date=21 July 2011}}</ref> along with captain [[Stephen McManus]]<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/spl/celtic/celtic-3-5m-up-on-summer-deals-as-stephen-mcmanus-checks-out-with-efrain-juarez-poised-to-check-in-1.1041148 |title=Celtic £3.5m up on summer deals as Stephen McManus checks out with Efrain Juarez poised to check in|work=Herald Scotland|date=13 July 2010|access-date=18 July 2011}}</ref> and fan favourite [[Artur Boruc]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/8812754.stm|title= Goalkeeper Artur Boruc moves to Fiorentina from Celtic |work=BBC Sport|date=15 July 2010|access-date=18 July 2011}}</ref> 13 other players also left the club, this gave Lennon enough funds to re-build for the new season. He looked to sign talented, young, cheap, relatively unknown players, from smaller leagues around the world.<ref name=signingpolicy /> This paid off with players such as [[Gary Hooper]],<ref>{{cite news|title=Scunthorpe striker Gary Hooper signs for Celtic|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/8852050.stm|work=BBC Sport|access-date=17 July 2011|date=27 July 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Scunthorpe's Gary Hooper completes £2.5m move to Celtic|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/27/gary-hooper-celtic|work=The Guardian|access-date=17 July 2011|date=27 July 2010}}</ref> [[Beram Kayal]]<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic sign Israel midfielder Beram Kayal|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/8849942.stm|work=BBC Sport|access-date=17 July 2011|date=29 July 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic complete signing of Israeli midfielder Biram Kayal|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/29/celtic-transfer-israeli-midfielder-biram-kayal|work=The Guardian|access-date=17 July 2011|date=29 July 2010}}</ref> and [[Emilio Izaguirre]]<ref>{{cite web|title=Celtic sign Honduran international defender Emilio Izaguirre|url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/football/spl/2010/08/18/celtic-sign-honduran-international-defender-emilio-izaguirre-86908-22496304/|work=Daily Record|access-date=17 July 2011|date=18 August 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic complete deal for left-back Emilio Izaguirre|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/8925044.stm|work=BBC Sport|access-date=17 July 2011|date=18 August 2010}}</ref> all having excellent seasons and earning many plaudits.<ref name=signingpolicy>{{cite news |url=http://sport.stv.tv/football/scottish-premier/celtic/261423-neil-lennon-explains-celtics-recruitment-policy/ |title=Neil Lennon explains Celtic's recruitment policy |work=STV Sport |date=6 July 2011 |access-date=18 July 2011 |archive-date=11 July 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110711043401/http://sport.stv.tv/football/scottish-premier/celtic/261423-neil-lennon-explains-celtics-recruitment-policy/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> Lennon also signed several experienced players on free transfers. [[Charlie Mulgrew]],<ref>{{cite web|last=Ananth|first=Adithya|title=Celtic Sign Charlie Mulgrew From Aberdeen on a Free|url=http://www.goal.com/en/news/1863/world-cup-2010/2010/07/01/2004475/celtic-sign-charlie-mulgrew-from-aberdeen-on-a-free|work=Goal.com|access-date=17 July 2011|date=1 July 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Defender Charlie Mulgrew seals second Celtic spell |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/8779638.stm|work=BBC Sport|access-date=17 July 2011|date=1 July 2010}}</ref> [[Joe Ledley]],<ref>{{cite news|title=Joe Ledley moves to Celtic to deepen Cardiff's financial crisis|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/07/joe-ledley-cardiff-celtic|work=The Guardian|access-date=17 July 2011|author=Stuart James, Ewan Murray|date=7 July 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Former Cardiff midfielder Joe Ledley signs for Celtic|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/8805917.stm|work=BBC Sport|access-date=17 July 2011|date=12 July 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic sign Joe Ledley from Cardiff but move for Jimmy Bullard stalls|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/jul/12/celtic-sign-joe-ledley-cardiff|work=The Guardian|date=12 July 2010}}</ref> and [[Daniel Majstorović]]<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic sign Swedish defender Daniel Majstorovic|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/8914013.stm|work=BBC Sport|access-date=17 July 2011|date=16 August 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic add experienced Sweden stopper Daniel Majstorovic to their ranks|url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/football/spl/2010/08/16/celtic-add-experienced-sweden-stopper-daniel-majstorovic-to-their-ranks-86908-22491694/|work=Daily Record|access-date=17 July 2011|date=16 August 2010}}</ref> all went into the first team. In addition to these Lennon also signed five other players, including [[Fraser Forster]]<ref>{{cite news|title=Celtic sign goalkeeper Fraser Forster on loan from Newcastle|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/aug/24/fraser-forster-joins-celtic-loan|work=The Guardian|author=Press Association|date=24 August 2010}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Newcastle goalkeeper Fraser Forster on Celtic loan|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/8935182.stm|work=BBC Sport|access-date=17 July 2011|date=24 August 2010}}</ref> on loan from [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle]] who became first choice goalkeeper and helped set a new [[Scottish Premier League|SPL]] record for most clean sheets.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.eveningtimes.co.uk/sport/editor-s-picks/fraser-forster-proud-of-role-in-shut-out-success-1.1102140 |title=Fraser Forster proud of role in shut-out success |work=Evening Times
|date=18 May 2011|access-date=18 July 2011}}</ref>

In July Celtic were drawn in the third qualifying round of the [[2010–11 UEFA Champions League|UEFA Champions League]] against Portuguese side [[S.C. Braga|Braga]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8829217.stm |title=Scottish clubs discover Euro foes |date=16 July 2010|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=18 July 2010| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100720065809/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/8829217.stm| archive-date= 20 July 2010 | url-status= live}}</ref> Celtic lost the first leg of the tie 3–0 away from home.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/8860029.stm |title=Braga 3 - 0 Celtic |date=28 July 2010|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=28 July 2010| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100728190241/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/8860029.stm| archive-date= 28 July 2010 | url-status= live}}</ref> Celtic won the return leg 2–1, but went out of the Champions League 4–2 on aggregate.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8879233.stm |title=Celtic 2 - 1 Braga |date=4 August 2010|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=4 August 2010| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100804185600/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/8879233.stm| archive-date= 4 August 2010 | url-status= live}}</ref> Celtic were knocked out of European football altogether in August, after they lost their [[2010–11 UEFA Europa League|Europa League]] qualifying match against [[FC Utrecht]] 4–2 on aggregate.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/europe/8943575.stm |title=FC Utrecht 4 - 0 Celtic (agg 4 - 2) |date=26 August 2010|publisher=BBC Sport|access-date=27 August 2010| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20100827111935/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/europe/8943575.stm| archive-date= 27 August 2010 | url-status= live}}</ref>

Celtic won their first eight league games of the [[2010–11 Scottish Premier League|SPL season]],<ref name=CRAIH>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/scot_prem/9122150.stm Celtic v Rangers as it happened] BBC Sport, 24 October 2010</ref> before losing to Rangers, who also had a 100% record, 3–1.<ref name=CRAIH/> Celtic started November beating [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] [[Celtic 9–0 Aberdeen|9–0]] in an SPL record victory.<ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/football/2010/nov/06/celtic-aberdeen-scottish-premier-league Celtic hit nine past Aberdeen in record SPL victory] guardian.co.uk, 6 November 2010</ref> They then lost to [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Heart of Midlothian]]<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_prem/9172846.stm Hearts 2-0 Celtic] BBC Sport, 10 November 2010</ref> and drew at home against [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee United]]<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_prem/9207786.stm Celtic 1 - 1 Dundee Utd] BBC Sport, 20 November 2010
</ref> and [[Inverness Caledonian Thistle F.C.|Inverness Caledonian Thistle]],<ref name=Inv222010>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_prem/9230565.stm Celtic 2 - 2 Inverness CT] BBC Sport, 27 November 2010</ref> with a victory over St Mirren the only consolation.<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_prem/9182198.stm St Mirren 0 - 1 Celtic] BBC Sport, 14 November 2010</ref> The Inverness match was notable because it was officiated by [[Luxembourg]] referee [[Alain Hamer]],<ref name=Inv222010/> who had stepped in because of the [[Scottish football referee strike]].<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/scotland/9235676.stm Scottish Football Association aim to support referees] BBC Sport, 28 November 2010</ref> Celtic began 2011 by beating [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] 2–0 at [[Ibrox Stadium|Ibrox]],<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/c/celtic/9333600.stm Celtic's Neil Lennon hails unplayable Georgios Samaras] BBC Sport, 2 January 2011</ref> and beat their rivals again 3–0 on 20 February.<ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/feb/20/celtic-rangers-scottish-premier-league Celtic enjoy breathing space at the top after beating dismal Rangers] guardian.co.uk, 20 February 2011</ref> Celtic finished the season in second place in the SPL table, a point behind Rangers.<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_prem/9484608.stm Celtic 4 - 0 Motherwell] BBC Sport, 15 May 2011</ref>

In the [[2010–11 Scottish League Cup|League Cup]], Celtic beat [[Inverness Caledonian Thistle F.C.|Inverness]] 6–0<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/9013893.stm Celtic 6 - 0 Inverness CT] BBC Sport, 22 September 2010</ref> and [[St Johnstone F.C.|St Johnstone]] 3–2<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/9122491.stm St Johnstone 2 - 3 Celtic] BBC Sport, 27 October 2010</ref> before beating [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] in the semi-final.<ref>[http://sport.scotsman.com/celticfc/Celtic-4--1-Aberdeen.6706111.jp Celtic 4 - 1 Aberdeen: Celtic book place in cup final] Scotsman.com Sport, 29 January 2011</ref> In the final, they lost 2–1 to Rangers [[overtime (association football)|after extra time]].<ref>[http://www.mirrorfootball.co.uk/news/Celtic-1-2-Rangers-Nikica-Jelavic-scores-extra-time-winner-to-claim-Scottish-Cup-article715819.html Celtic 1-2 Rangers: Jelavic scores extra time winner] MirrorFootball, 20 March 2011</ref> In the [[2010–11 Scottish Cup|Scottish Cup]], Celtic entered in the fourth round, beating [[Scottish Football League Third Division|Third Division]] team [[Berwick Rangers F.C.|Berwick Rangers]] 2–0,<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/scot_cups/9350659.stm Berwick 0 - 2 Celtic] BBC Sport, 9 January 2011</ref> and drawing 2–2 with Rangers in the fifth round.<ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/feb/06/rangers-celtic-scottish-cup El Hadji Diouf stays calm as Celtic haul back Rangers in Scottish Cup] guardian.co.uk, 6 February 2011</ref> They beat Rangers 1–0 in the replay<ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/mar/02/celtic-rangers-scottish-cup-report El-Hadji Diouf's sending off sparks mayhem at Old Firm cup tie] guardian.co.uk, 2 March 2011</ref> and booked their place in the final beating Aberdeen 4–0 at [[Hampden Park]].<ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/apr/17/celtic-aberdeen-scottish-cup-semi-final Celtic march into Scottish Cup final against 10-man Aberdeen] guardian.co.uk, 17 April 2011</ref> In the final Celtic beat [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] 3–0, with goals scored by [[Ki Sung-Yeung]], Charlie Mulgrew and an [[own goal]] from [[Stephen Craigan]].<ref>[https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/celtic/8528267/Scottish-Cup-final-2011-Celtic-3-Motherwell-0-match-report.html Scottish Cup final 2011: Celtic 3 Motherwell 0 match report] The Telegraph, 21 May 2011</ref>

Celtic played in an unprecedented seven Old Firm matches in 2010–11.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.dailyrecord.co.uk/football/spl/rangers/2011/07/19/i-hope-old-firm-battle-this-season-will-be-every-bit-as-enthralling-as-last-says-andy-goram-86908-23281249/|title=I hope Old Firm battle this season will be every bit as enthralling as last, says Rangers legend Andy Goram|publisher=Daily Record|date=19 July 2011|access-date=20 July 2011}}</ref> This was due to being drawn against Rangers in both cups, and the fact that they required a replay to beat them in the [[2010–11 Scottish Cup|Scottish Cup]]. Celtic won three of these matches, two in the league and the Scottish Cup fifth round replay. There were two draws, one in the final league match and one in the Scottish Cup fifth round. Rangers won twice, both the first league match and the League Cup final. Celtic also enjoyed an incredible run of results against Aberdeen. The two teams played each other five times, due to being drawn in both Cup semi-finals and Celtic scored 21 times against them only conceding one.<ref>{{cite web|title=Result Record by Team (2010-2011)|url=http://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/team_record_by_team.php?from=2&to=2&competition=0&venue=0&update=Update|publisher=FitbaStats|access-date=2 March 2015}}</ref>

